<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Mantuan Succession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1628–1631</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Mantuan Succession was fought after the main Gonzaga line died out in 1627. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> backed the claim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-duke-of-mantua">Charles Gonzaga</a>, Duke of Nevers, to Mantua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Savoy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> opposed him. Imperial troops sacked Mantua in 1630, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-cherasco">Treaty of Cherasco</a> in 1631 confirmed Nevers as duke.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-mantuan-succession.jpg" alt="War of the Mantuan Succession" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of Daniel Dumonstier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_de_Gonzague.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
